for (const [initializerIdentifier, componentIdentifiers] of Object.entries(jsComponentInitializers)) {
  const initializerFunc = DotNet.findJSFunction(initializerIdentifier, 0) as JSComponentInitializerCallback;
  for (const componentIdentifier of componentIdentifiers)
    initializerFunc(componentIdentifier, jsComponentParameters[componentIdentifier]);
}

if (manager && currentRendererId === rendererId) {
    // A different renderer type (e.g., Server vs WebAssembly) is trying to enable JS root components.
    // This is a multi-host scenario which is not supported for dynamic root components.
    throw new Error('Dynamic root components have already been enabled.');
  }

// When the same renderer type re-enables (e.g., circuit restart or new circuit on same page),
  // accept the new manager. The old manager's DotNetObjectReference is no longer valid anyway
  // because the old circuit is gone. We don't dispose the old manager - doing so would cause
  // JSDisconnectedException because the circuit that created it no longer exists.
  currentRendererId = rendererId;
  manager = managerInstance;

[Theory]
[InlineData("ServerNonPrerendered")]
[InlineData("WebAssemblyNonPrerendered")]
public void PersistentStateIsSupportedInDynamicJSRoots(string renderMode)
{
    Navigate($"subdir/WasmMinimal/dynamic-js-root.html?renderMode={renderMode}");

    Browser.Equal("Counter", () => Browser.Exists(By.TagName("h1")).Text);
    Browser.Equal("Current count: 0", () => Browser.Exists(By.CssSelector("p[role='status']")).Text);

    Browser.Click(By.CssSelector("button.btn-primary"));
    Browser.Equal("Current count: 1", () => Browser.Exists(By.CssSelector("p[role='status']")).Text);
}

findings.body:
The condition throws when the incoming `rendererId` **matches** the stored one:

::diff{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="126-130"}

Take the restart scenario from the PR description. Circuit 1 enables with `rendererId = WebRendererId.Server (1)`, then circuit 2 calls `enableJSRootComponents(1, newManager, ...)`. `manager` is set and `currentRendererId === 1`, so the call throws `Dynamic root components have already been enabled.` That is the same failure the PR is supposed to fix (dotnet/aspnetcore#64523).

In the multi-host case it's the other way round. Server (1) enables first, then WebAssembly (2) calls in. `1 === 2` is false, so nothing throws. The WASM manager silently replaces the Server manager, and `currentRendererId` flips to 2.

The comment inside the block ("A different renderer type ... is trying to enable") describes `!==`, not what the code checks.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="126-130"}
  if (manager && currentRendererId !== rendererId) {
    // A different renderer type (e.g., Server vs WebAssembly) is trying to enable JS root components.
    // This is a multi-host scenario which is not supported for dynamic root components.
    throw new Error('Dynamic root components have already been enabled.');
  }

::callout{type="warning"}
Now that `RazorComponentEndpointsStartup` always registers a JS root component, every Server app in the E2E suite goes through this path. Any test that starts a second circuit on the same page (pause/resume, circuit restart after a failed reconnect) will hit this exception.

The rewrite dropped the line `jsComponentParametersByIdentifier = jsComponentParameters;`. Only `currentRendererId` and `manager` are set now:

::diff{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="136-150"}

The module variable (line 14) stays `undefined`, but `add()` still reads it right after the .NET call:

::code{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="29-31"}

So every `Blazor.rootComponents.add(...)` call fails with `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading '<identifier>')`. That includes the first circuit, not only restarts. Worse, it fails *after* `AddRootComponent` has already created a component on the .NET side, so that root component is left orphaned. `dynamic-js-root.html` calls `add()`, so `PersistentStateIsSupportedInDynamicJSRoots` should fail as well.

Restore the assignment. It also needs to run on re-enable (outside the `hasInitializedJsComponents` block):

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Web.JS/src/Rendering/JSRootComponents.ts" lines="136-137"}
  currentRendererId = rendererId;
  manager = managerInstance;
  jsComponentParametersByIdentifier = jsComponentParameters;

The only test touched here is the one moved into `StatePersistenceTest`. It loads the page once and clicks a counter, so it never re-enables JS root components:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/Tests/StatePersistenceTest.cs" lines="279-291"}

Nothing exercises the scenario in the PR title: a second `enableJSRootComponents` call from the same renderer type after a circuit restart. A test for it would have caught the reversed guard right away. Also, the moved test sits in a class whose fixture has no `--RegisterDynamicJSRootComponent` argument, so it depends on the unconditional registration now in `RazorComponentEndpointsStartup`. That's fine, but it means the rest of the suite runs with JS root components enabled too (see the next finding).

Registering `dynamic-js-root-counter` is no longer behind a config flag:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/testassets/Components.TestServer/RazorComponentEndpointsStartup.cs" lines="54-54"}

Every Server and WebAssembly renderer in every E2E test built on this startup will now call `enableJSRootComponents`. Before, none of them did. That widens the impact of bugs in this code path: with the reversed guard, any test that restarts a circuit on the same page will fail with an unrelated-looking error. It's a reasonable way to get broad coverage of the restart fix, but call it out, and run the whole Components E2E suite before merging, not just the moved test.
